<p>You are standing in a kitchen. The word for <em>colander</em> in your target language arrives instantly, unasked, because the object is on the shelf in front of you. Forty minutes later you are at a market stall trying to ask for one, and the word is simply not there. Not slow. Not approximate. Absent.</p>

<p>The standard explanation is that you never really learned it. That explanation is comfortable, moralizing, and mostly wrong. What happened in that kitchen was not a memory that failed. It was a memory that stayed where it was made. The shelf was part of the memory, and you walked away from the shelf.</p>

<p>Psychologists have studied this for fifty years under the name <strong>context-dependent memory</strong>, and the literature is unusually useful for language learners because it contains both a famous success and a famous failure. Understanding why the same experiment produced opposite results thirty years apart tells you exactly when to trust the room and when to stop trusting it.</p>

<h2>What Encoding Specificity Actually Claims</h2>

<p>The theoretical root of the whole field is Tulving and Thomson's 1973 paper in <em>Psychological Review</em>, "Encoding specificity and retrieval processes in episodic memory." The claim is narrow and it is not about rooms. It is that <strong>what you can retrieve depends on the overlap between the information stored with the memory and the information present at the moment of retrieval.</strong> A cue works to the extent that it was part of the original encoding. A cue that is new at test, however vivid, may do nothing.</p>

<p>Applied to vocabulary, this reframes a common complaint. When a learner says "I know this word but I can't produce it," what they usually mean is that they know it under one set of cues and are being asked for it under another. The word was encoded while reading, with the printed form in front of them and the meaning supplied by a gloss. It is now being requested from speech, with no print, no gloss, and no time. Those are not the same task. The memory exists. The key changed.</p>

<h2>The Divers, and the Replication That Didn't Work</h2>

<p>The experiment every textbook cites is Godden and Baddeley's 1975 study in the <em>British Journal of Psychology</em>, volume 66, pages 325 to 331. Scuba divers learned lists of words either on dry land or underwater, then recalled them either on land or underwater. The design gives four conditions: land-land, land-water, water-land, water-water.</p>

<p>The published result became one of the most quoted findings in memory research. Recall was substantially better when the environment at test matched the environment at learning, in both directions. Learn underwater, recall underwater, and the words came back more readily than when the same words were tested on the beach. The practical moral seemed obvious: context is glued to memory, so keep the context.</p>

<p>Then in 2021, Murre published a replication in <em>Royal Society Open Science</em> (volume 8, article 200724) with sixteen divers, each of whom completed all four combinations, tested four minutes after study. The abstract states the outcome without flinching:</p>

<blockquote>Contrary to the original study, we did not find that recall in the same context where the words had been learned was better than recall in the other context.</blockquote>

<p>Two possibilities are worth holding at once. Either the original effect was overstated, or the effect is real but depends on conditions the replication did not reproduce. The meta-analytic evidence points at the second answer, and the conditions turn out to be the interesting part.</p>

<h2>The Meta-Analysis: Real, But Fragile</h2>

<p>Smith and Vela's 2001 review and meta-analysis in <em>Psychonomic Bulletin and Review</em>, volume 8, pages 203 to 220, pooled the incidental environmental context literature and reported that across all studies, environmental context effects were reliable. So the effect exists. But the same paper explains why it so often evaporates, using two mechanisms with excellent names.</p>

<h3>Overshadowing: better cues at study</h3>

<p>If the material you are learning is rich enough to supply its own retrieval cues, the room never gets encoded in the first place. Smith and Vela's term is <strong>overshadowing</strong>: noncontextual cues present during learning reduce the effect of environmental manipulations. A diver memorizing a bare word list has nothing else to hold onto, so the water becomes part of the memory. A learner working through an example sentence, an image, and an audio clip has three anchors already. The room is redundant, so it is not recorded, so changing rooms costs nothing.</p>

<h3>Outshining: better cues at test</h3>

<p>The mirror mechanism operates at retrieval. If the test itself provides strong cues, the environment is drowned out. Recognition tasks, where the answer is on the page in front of you, are much less context-sensitive than free recall tasks, where you must generate the answer from nothing. And the third finding is the practical one: <strong>mentally reinstating the context at test reduces the effect of the environment</strong>, because you have supplied the cue yourself rather than depending on the room to supply it.</p>

<p>That single sentence is the difference between a learner who is trapped by their study location and a learner who is not. The room is a cue. If you can generate the cue, you do not need the room.</p>

<h2>The Classroom Study You Can Copy Tonight</h2>

<p>Grant and colleagues, publishing in <em>Applied Cognitive Psychology</em> in 1998 (volume 12, pages 617 to 623), ran the version of this experiment that speaks directly to students. Forty participants read an article in either silent or noisy conditions, then were tested on comprehension in either silent or noisy conditions, using two standard academic formats: short answer, which is recall, and multiple choice, which is recognition.</p>

<blockquote>Both tests showed context-dependency effects in which performance was better in the matching conditions (silent study/silent test and noisy study/noisy test) than in the mismatching conditions (silent study/noisy test and noisy study/silent test).</blockquote>

<p>Notice what this does to the obvious objection. Context effects are supposed to be a laboratory curiosity that dies when the material is meaningful. Here the material was a real article, the tests were real exam formats, and the context was ordinary noise. The effect survived all three. The authors' own academic recommendation was that students may do better by studying in silence, since exams tend to be silent.</p>

<p>For language learners the transferable point is smaller and more useful than "study in silence." It is that <strong>the conditions you practice in become part of what you practiced</strong>, and a mismatch is a real cost, not a moral failing.</p>

<h2>The Result That Argues the Other Way</h2>

<p>So far the advice would be: pick one perfect room and never leave it. The next study says something better. Smith and Rothkopf, in <em>Cognition and Instruction</em> in 1984 (volume 1, pages 341 to 358), taught an eight-hour statistics course as four videotaped lessons, then tested retention five days later in a completely new environment.</p>

<p>They varied two things. The lessons were either massed into a single day or distributed over four days. And the environment was either held constant in one room or diversified by teaching each of the four lessons in a different room. The finding:</p>

<blockquote>Diversification of context by using a different room for each of the four lessons resulted in better productive performance for field-dependent students.</blockquote>

<p>Two details make this study matter. First, it helped rather than hurt, which is the opposite of what a naive reading of context-dependence would predict. Second, the authors checked performance at the end of each lesson and found no difference between conditions. The advantage appeared only at the delayed test. Their conclusion was that these were <strong>retrieval</strong> effects, not learning effects: nothing had been learned better, but more routes back to the material had been built.</p>

<p>Put the two findings together and the practical rule falls out almost mechanically. A single fixed context makes retrieval easy in that context and brittle everywhere else, because every memory hangs on one hook. Many contexts make each individual retrieval slightly harder and leave the material attached to many hooks, so any one of them can bring it back. The room you never change is a crutch. The rooms you keep changing are training.</p>

<h2>Language Is a Context Too</h2>

<p>For anyone learning a second language there is a version of this effect that is more consequential than furniture. Marian and Neisser, in the <em>Journal of Experimental Psychology: General</em> in 2000 (volume 129, pages 361 to 368), interviewed Russian-English bilinguals about their lives and varied the language of the interview and the language of the word prompts independently. Memories were more accessible when the language at retrieval matched the language at encoding. Participants retrieved more experiences from their Russian-speaking years when interviewed in Russian, and more from their English-speaking years when interviewed in English.</p>

<p>The abstract's closing line is the one to keep:</p>

<blockquote>These findings show that language at the time of retrieval, like other forms of context, plays a significant role in determining what will be remembered.</blockquote>

<p>Now apply that to a flashcard. Every card you build with an English gloss on the front has been encoded inside an English context. The retrieval route you rehearsed runs through English. Then you stand in a shop in Lisbon and the route you need runs through Portuguese only, with no English anywhere in the cue. You are not being tested on what you studied. You are being tested on a different task, in a different context, and the mismatch is invisible because both tasks feel like "knowing the word."</p>

<p>This is also the mechanism behind a familiar expatriate experience: the vocabulary you learned in a classroom in your home country behaves differently from the vocabulary you picked up in-country, even when the words are identical. They were stored with different cues, and they come back on different occasions.</p>

<h2>A Five-Step Protocol for Cues That Travel</h2>

<p>None of this requires a laboratory. It requires deciding, once, that the room will not be allowed to carry your vocabulary.</p>

<h3>1. Rotate the location deliberately</h3>

<p>Review the same deck in at least three physically different places each week: a desk, a kitchen, a train, a queue. Do not optimize for comfort. The point is to prevent any single environment from becoming the only route. Expect the sessions in new places to feel worse. That feeling is the training.</p>

<h3>2. Match the retrieval mode to the use</h3>

<p>If you will need the word in speech, retrieve it in speech, out loud, from an audio or written prompt. If you will need it in writing, write it. Silent recognition practice builds a route that only works when the answer is already visible, which is the one condition real life never provides.</p>

<h3>3. Put the cue inside the card</h3>

<p>This is the overshadowing principle used on purpose. A card with a target-language example sentence, a small image, and an audio clip carries its own context, so the room becomes irrelevant by design. The front of the card should contain the smallest cue that still makes the answer findable, and that cue should live in the target language as often as possible.</p>

<h3>4. Add one "wrong conditions" test per week</h3>

<p>Take ten cards you know well and review them somewhere loud, standing up, with no desk, in ninety seconds. Anything that survives has more than one hook. Anything that fails tells you precisely which memories are still tied to a chair.</p>

<h3>5. Rehearse the reinstatement, not the room</h3>

<p>Smith and Vela found that mentally reinstating context at test reduces dependence on the actual environment. Before an exam or a conversation, spend twenty seconds recalling the specific study scene: the page, the sentence, the sound of the word. That voluntary reconstruction is the cue, and unlike a room it comes with you.</p>

<h2>The Uncomfortable Part</h2>

<p>The reason context-dependent memory is worth understanding is that it removes an excuse and replaces it with a diagnosis. When a word fails in the world, the failure is not evidence that you are untalented or that the word was too hard. It is evidence that the word was filed under the wrong heading: filed under <code>chapter 4, page 61, Tuesday evening, at my desk</code> rather than filed under its own meaning.</p>

<p>The diving experiment is remembered because it is dramatic, and the failed replication is more useful than the success, because it shows what the effect is made of. Divers with nothing but a word list lean on the water. Learners with sentences, sounds, and images do not need to. The room was never holding the word. It was only ever one of the hooks, and hooks can be replaced.</p>

